{
  "description": "A family-aware diagnostic adapter for Self-EvolveRec that measures a trained GRU4Rec model through the host ModelView interface and produces an interpretation prompt grounded in measured metrics and their definitions.",
  "motivation": "Support model improvement using reproducible diagnostics while preserving the GRU4Rec backbone, benchmark protocol, dataset routing, and single-model constraint.",
  "implementation_notes": "Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). The loader supplies (None, sequence, positive, negative), with tensors shaped B × L. Preserve item_emb, log2feats(sequence), and predict_score(features, items). For left-padded histories, read the final nonpadding position rather than count−1. Use only observed histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps; treat unavailable review data and simulator traits as unobserved. Route each category to exactly one independently trained checkpoint. Select improvements by macro validation HR@10 across all four categories. The supplied description contains no measured results or complete probe definitions.",
  "pseudocode": "Initialize DiagnosisProbe(view, device).\nSet view.model to evaluation mode and disable gradients.\nFor each loader batch:\n  Extract sequence, positive, and negative tensors.\n  Obtain family-compatible features with view.log2feats(sequence).\n  Exclude padding from applicable measurements.\n  Score items through view.predict_score(features, items).\n  Accumulate explicitly defined diagnostic measurements.\nReturn {metrics, metric_definitions}.\nConstruct diagnosis_interpreter_prompt from those measurements and definitions, identifying missing evidence and avoiding unsupported causal claims.",
  "originality": {
    "score": 3,
    "positive": "Connects family-specific sequential-model diagnostics to an automated improvement workflow.",
    "negative": "Primarily an interface and evaluation specification; it introduces no explicit new diagnostic algorithm. Score uses a 1–10 scale."
  },
  "future_potential": {
    "score": 7,
    "positive": "Could support targeted GRU4Rec improvements and comparable diagnosis across categories.",
    "negative": "Its usefulness depends on defining reliable probes and demonstrating that their findings predict validation improvements. Score uses a 1–10 scale."
  },
  "code_difficulty": {
    "score": 5,
    "positive": "The required interfaces and batch format provide a clear implementation boundary.",
    "negative": "Correct padding handling, metric aggregation, architecture compatibility, and checkpoint–predictor consistency require care. Score uses a 1–10 scale, where higher means harder."
  }
}
